{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgFAKD4ACwPpCzWeEb5HKS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n5\t- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n6\t- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n7\t\n8\t## Title\n9\tReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n12\t\n13\t**Claim 1 — Item Dominance (benchmark-conditional)**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in multiple-choice LLM evaluation (MVP: 58.4% on MMLU single-model; expected to decrease with model-as-facet reanalysis). Its magnitude is hypothesized to be benchmark-dependent — weakening on reasoning tasks where temperature/seed interactions increase — but this gradient requires exp-002 confirmation. Item dominance is expected from measurement theory when item difficulty varies widely (Brennan, 2001); our contribution is in (a) quantifying the precise magnitude across benchmarks and models, (b) demonstrating whether a systematic benchmark-dependent gradient exists, and (c) deriving D-study implications specific to LLM evaluation.\n14\t**Fallback narratives**: (A) If gradient confirmed → \"benchmark-conditional variance structure\"; (B) If gradient not significant → \"item dominance is universal but magnitude varies\"; (C) If model-as-facet reduces item_id below 30% → pivot to \"model×item interaction is the dominant source\" — narrative direction changes substantially.\n15\t\n16\t**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n17\t\n18\t**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent: binary correct scoring yields G=0.357 (item dominance makes G≥0.90 unreachable without massive item expansion), while text-exact-match scoring yields G=0.975 (already adequate). D-study projections show that increasing item sampling is more cost-effective than increasing prompt/seed replication, but the optimal allocation depends critically on both the evaluation metric and the benchmark. Current common practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items, single metric) produce misleading reliability estimates.\n19\t\n20\t## Contributions (3 条，明确区分)\n21\t\n22\t1. **Cross-benchmark variance gradient**: The first multi-benchmark G-theory analysis showing that variance structure systematically shifts from item-dominated (knowledge retrieval) to interaction-dominated (reasoning tasks), explaining why prior single-benchmark studies reached seemingly contradictory conclusions.\n23\t\n24\t2. **Confirmatory separability test**: Infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible at the aggregate level (<0.5% under binary scoring), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies — with the caveat that both factors interact with items and the finding is metric-conditional.\n25\t\n26\t3. **Task-specific reproducibility budgets**: D-study projections with bootstrap CIs yield actionable, benchmark-specific item/prompt/seed allocations for achieving target reliability (G≥0.90), contextualized against IRT-based subsampling approaches (tinyBenchmarks, HELM-Lite).\n27\t\n28\t## Story Spine\n29\t\n30\t**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n31\t\n32\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n33\t\n34\t**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n35\t\n36\t**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of K conditions across M models and N benchmarks, item selection is the largest single variance source on multiple-choice benchmarks (X-Y%, benchmark-dependent [placeholder: fill after exp-001/002]), while direct infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible. Variance structure [is/is not] benchmark-conditional [pending exp-002]. D-study reproducibility budgets show that optimal facet allocation is both benchmark-specific and metric-conditional.\"\n37\t\n38\t## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n39\t\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n40\t\n41\t## Narrative Strategy\n42\t- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n43\t- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n44\t- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n45\t- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n46\t- **Figure plan** (4 figures minimum):\n47\t  1. Problem illustration: same model, different benchmark → different dominant variance source\n48\t  2. G-theory facet diagram showing crossed design\n49\t  3. Stacked-bar variance decomposition across benchmarks (signature figure)\n50\t  4. D-study projection curves: G vs item count for different benchmarks\n51\t- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n52\t  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n53\t  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n54\t  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n55\t  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n56\t\n57\t## Venue Fit Risks\n58\t- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n59\t- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n60\t- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n61\t- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n62\t\n63\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n64\t1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n65\t2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n66\t3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n67\t4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n68\t5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n69\t6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n70\t7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n71\t8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n72\t9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n73\t10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n74\t11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n75\t12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n76\t13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n77\t14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n78\t15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n79\t\n80\t## Design Decisions\n81\t- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n82\t- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n83\t- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n84\t- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n85\t- **Model as crossed facet**: In exp-001 analysis (exp-004), model is treated as a crossed random facet in G-theory, not pooled or stratified. This properly attributes model×item interaction and avoids inflating item_id estimates.\n86\t- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n87\t- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n88\t\n89\t## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n90\t\n91\t### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n92\t**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n93\t**Defense strategy**: (a) Report correlation between Bernoulli variance p_i(1-p_i) and observed item variance — quantify how much item_id is \"mechanical\" vs informative; (b) Run parallel analysis on text_exact_match (continuous metric) to show variance structure is robust across metrics; (c) Discuss generalized G-theory (Brennan 2001 Ch.12) in Limitations; (d) Cite Lalor et al. (2019) who used IRT (also assumes latent continuous) on binary NLP data without this criticism being fatal.\n94\t\n95\t### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n96\t**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n97\t**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n98\t\n99\t### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n100\t**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n101\t**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n102\t\n103\t### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n104\t**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n105\t**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n106\t\n107\t### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n108\t**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n109\t**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n110\t\n111\t### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n112\t**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n113\t**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n114\t\n115\t### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n116\t**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n117\t**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n118\t\n119\t### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n120\t**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n121\t**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n122\t\n123\t### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n124\t**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n125\t**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n126\t\n127\t## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\n128\tKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n129\t- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n130\t- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n131\t- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n132\t- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n133\t- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n134\t- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n135\t- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n136\t- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical)\n137\t- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n138\t- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n139\t- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n140\t- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n141\t- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n142\t- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n143\t- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18b268ca-478e-4248-84eb-ba86eb305b05","uuid":"a817781e-0977-4373-b5ae-71faaf7a278a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:11:40.873Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032813/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n\n## Title\nReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 — Item Dominance (benchmark-conditional)**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in multiple-choice LLM evaluation (MVP: 58.4% on MMLU single-model; expected to decrease with model-as-facet reanalysis). Its magnitude is hypothesized to be benchmark-dependent — weakening on reasoning tasks where temperature/seed interactions increase — but this gradient requires exp-002 confirmation. Item dominance is expected from measurement theory when item difficulty varies widely (Brennan, 2001); our contribution is in (a) quantifying the precise magnitude across benchmarks and models, (b) demonstrating whether a systematic benchmark-dependent gradient exists, and (c) deriving D-study implications specific to LLM evaluation.\n**Fallback narratives**: (A) If gradient confirmed → \"benchmark-conditional variance structure\"; (B) If gradient not significant → \"item dominance is universal but magnitude varies\"; (C) If model-as-facet reduces item_id below 30% → pivot to \"model×item interaction is the dominant source\" — narrative direction changes substantially.\n\n**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n\n**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent: binary correct scoring yields G=0.357 (item dominance makes G≥0.90 unreachable without massive item expansion), while text-exact-match scoring yields G=0.975 (already adequate). D-study projections show that increasing item sampling is more cost-effective than increasing prompt/seed replication, but the optimal allocation depends critically on both the evaluation metric and the benchmark. Current common practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items, single metric) produce misleading reliability estimates.\n\n## Contributions (3 条，明确区分)\n\n1. **Cross-benchmark variance gradient**: The first multi-benchmark G-theory analysis showing that variance structure systematically shifts from item-dominated (knowledge retrieval) to interaction-dominated (reasoning tasks), explaining why prior single-benchmark studies reached seemingly contradictory conclusions.\n\n2. **Confirmatory separability test**: Infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible at the aggregate level (<0.5% under binary scoring), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies — with the caveat that both factors interact with items and the finding is metric-conditional.\n\n3. **Task-specific reproducibility budgets**: D-study projections with bootstrap CIs yield actionable, benchmark-specific item/prompt/seed allocations for achieving target reliability (G≥0.90), contextualized against IRT-based subsampling approaches (tinyBenchmarks, HELM-Lite).\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n\n**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of K conditions across M models and N benchmarks, item selection is the largest single variance source on multiple-choice benchmarks (X-Y%, benchmark-dependent [placeholder: fill after exp-001/002]), while direct infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible. Variance structure [is/is not] benchmark-conditional [pending exp-002]. D-study reproducibility budgets show that optimal facet allocation is both benchmark-specific and metric-conditional.\"\n\n## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n\n## Narrative Strategy\n- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n- **Figure plan** (4 figures minimum):\n  1. Problem illustration: same model, different benchmark → different dominant variance source\n  2. G-theory facet diagram showing crossed design\n  3. Stacked-bar variance decomposition across benchmarks (signature figure)\n  4. D-study projection curves: G vs item count for different benchmarks\n- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n\n## Design Decisions\n- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n- **Model as crossed facet**: In exp-001 analysis (exp-004), model is treated as a crossed random facet in G-theory, not pooled or stratified. This properly attributes model×item interaction and avoids inflating item_id estimates.\n- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n\n## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n\n### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n**Defense strategy**: (a) Report correlation between Bernoulli variance p_i(1-p_i) and observed item variance — quantify how much item_id is \"mechanical\" vs informative; (b) Run parallel analysis on text_exact_match (continuous metric) to show variance structure is robust across metrics; (c) Discuss generalized G-theory (Brennan 2001 Ch.12) in Limitations; (d) Cite Lalor et al. (2019) who used IRT (also assumes latent continuous) on binary NLP data without this criticism being fatal.\n\n### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n\n### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n\n### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n\n### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n\n### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n\n### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n\n### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n\n### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n\n## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\nKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical)\n- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n","numLines":144,"startLine":1,"totalLines":144}}}
